" "q-119300/selection/9b69f980fc5ccabbe990fc77bf5b0d0e464e92f3fb37726c43772ebfb71f41b0" "q-119300/selection/d5e0ace31c5206013cb6fdaeb399808363636e58d1bf8c7bad9281a9ab544206" "q-119300/selection/1eab6dbdfe252599e4657a18c5acf04fc959b1f3c9c7d1b2bdb1c249d1f65bc1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-119300" :question/id #uuid "381c6867-01dd-581f-aef2-bf68fe1db768" :question/math-academy-id "q-119300" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/4dc193655982bcb9b5281b20814402e85ba1aeda39769e256b74a95a47ba6db4.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/45a0f2e8b0c46e8b9a2985ff20c42457cd617957cd3da168a0c91171aceacfb2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-119300/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-119300"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96167"] :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go left and right along the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/7715c9573d2a434bb5878ff3900632dae7121bd752bab6a9a7745f69de84f0ce.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8502"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/2d6f4a939a2c25d1706a7d54a323af2773d30ddb08bcfcada94466bb8d1b9ef9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=2(2)-1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/be6bc036e194683af09a5936d433d7d45c7532e06054d9d473f3b433167ea102.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8503"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y = -1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x = 0,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n\n- At every point along the line $y = 0,$ the slope field is horizontal" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/f3ca77530d9126d733d33dc01ccee82d2740f789dcf712c67f03139fb09b753b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8504"] :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/3990c49349d446512d59f1f722506ea7337143eba6ec49f29dc87af060fb553b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/df3dd6c7db3e88ac2ac2a0ad82b19e5cac427f69ddf0eeba841445612e62ec4e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/4d518885f30e40ed870dba7b902139fca6ccbde81f7e468631c5199e887c4923.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
